  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Tonight: Moderate travel impacts from snow and ice mainly \n   south of the Highway 12 corridor.\n\n-  Another round of light valley snow Sunday night into Monday.\n\n\nAn approaching weather system is developing this afternoon, \ndriving increasing precipitation from west to east. Precipitation \nwill become widespread this evening from the Highway 200 corridor \nsouthward. Snow levels will begin between 5,000 and 6,000 feet \nthis afternoon before falling to near 3,000 feet, reaching \nwestern Montana valley floors and the Camas Prairie in north-\ncentral Idaho by early Saturday morning. Expect 3 to 7 inches of \nsnow across Lolo and Lost Trail passes as well as the backcountry \nof Idaho and Lemhi counties, with 2 to 5 inches along the travel \ncorridors of southwest Montana. Motorists should plan for slick \nroadways and allow extra travel time. Although precipitation will \ntaper off soon after snow levels reach many valleys, valley \nfloors could still receive up to an inch of snow, while \nsurrounding benches may pick up 1 to 2 inches.\n\nScattered showers will linger on Saturday under a cool, \nnorthwesterly flow with seasonable temperatures. As skies \npartially clear Saturday night into Sunday morning, patchy to \nareas of fog will become a concern, particularly in valleys.\n\nAnother wave of moisture arrives Sunday afternoon and persists \ninto early Tuesday. Across northwest Montana, snow levels may \nstart at valley floors and remain there through Monday morning, \ncreating the potential for light valley accumulations and slick \nroads. While overall precipitation amounts look light, mountain \npasses will once again face wintry travel impacts.",
